// A stack that runs out says so.
//
// A compiled program that recurses too deep walks off the bottom of
// its stack into the guard, which is mapped unreadable, and the kernel
// sends SIGSEGV.  Without a handler that is a bare "Segmentation
// fault" from the shell -- the one runtime stop this language did not
// report in its own voice.  With one, and with the guard's bounds to
// compare the faulting address against, the program says what
// happened and stops the way every other runtime stop does.
//
// The interpreter has no guard page to fault on; it runs out of
// Python's stack instead, and says the same sentence rather than
// Python's.
//
// Both runs print the prefix below and then fail, which is what a t9N
// file asks for.  What they say afterwards goes to the error stream.

fn down(n : i64) → i64:
    1 + down(n + 1)

@start
@impure
fn main():
    std.println("going down")
    std.println("{}", down(0))
